A view of the White House in Washington, DC, US, July 4, 2023.
—

The Justice Department is appealing a judge’s order prohibiting various Biden adm

**********Extracted claims**********

The Justice Department is appealing a judge’s order that prohibits various Biden administration agencies and officials from communicating with social media companies about certain content.
The Justice Department notified a court on Wednesday that it plans to appeal the preliminary injunction.
The preliminary injunction was issued by Trump-appointed US District Judge Terry Doughty on Tuesday.
The appeal will be made to the 5th US Circuit Court of Appeals.
Judge Doughty’s injunction orders federal agencies and top officials not to communicate with social media companies about taking down content containing protected free speech posted on the platforms.
Doughty’s order applies to agencies including the Department of Health and Human Services, the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the Justice Department, and the FBI.
Doughty’s order applies to officials such as US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy and White House press secretary Karine Jean-Pierre.
The injunction notes that the government can still communicate with social media companies as part of efforts to curb illegal activity and address national security threats.
The injunction prohibits agencies and officials from specifically flagging content or posts on social media platforms.
The government is prohibited by the injunction from forwarding content or posts to social media companies urging, encouraging, pressuring, or inducing removal, deletion, suppression, or reduction of content containing protected free speech.
The social media companies specified in the lawsuit include Facebook, Twitter, YouTube, WhatsApp, TikTok, and WeChat.
The order is part of a lawsuit brought in 2022 by Republican attorneys general from Missouri and Louisiana.
The lawsuit accuses the Biden administration of effectively silencing conservatives by leaning on social media companies to take down online misinformation about Covid-19.
A White House official on Tuesday defended the administration’s communication with social media companies.
The White House official stated that the administration’s communication has promoted responsible actions to protect public health, safety, and security when confronted by challenges.
The White House official stated that their consistent view is that social media platforms have a critical responsibility to take account of the effects their platforms are having on the American people, but they should make independent choices about the information they present.

